# work — the builder's manual

Every verb below is run as `work <verb>`. Generated from the verb definitions; it cannot drift from them.

## 1. Reading the work item
### work show
Prints your bead: description, labels, notes, children.
Use when: First, and again after any amendment or reopen.
Checks:
  - the bead is the bound one

### work prime
Prints this whole manual, generated from the code.
Use when: Before you begin.

### work help
Prints one verb's entry: what it does, its arguments, what it checks, and each refusal with its fix.
Use when: A verb refused you, or you are about to use one for the first time.
Arguments:
  <verb>
Checks:
  - the verb is one `work` serves
Refusals:
  - usage: work help <verb>
    Fix: name one verb; `work prime` lists them all
  - no verb {verb:?}
    Fix: run `work prime` for the verbs

## 2. Asking, noting, and being blocked
### work note
Leaves a plain note on your bead. No lifecycle effect.
Use when: You want evidence or a decision recorded where the next reader will see it.
Arguments:
  <text> | -   (text on stdin)
Checks:
  - the bead is the bound one

### work blocked
Holds your bead and files the ask in one step.
Use when: Only the operator can answer, and you cannot finish without it.
Arguments:
  "<the question>"
  --default "<what you would do by default>"
Checks:
  - the bead is the bound one
  - a default is given

### work ack
Records your answer to a live amendment of the bead.
Use when: `work show` lists an amendment awaiting your answer.
Arguments:
  <amendment-id>
  --accept | --decline (with a reason)
Checks:
  - the amendment belongs to your bead

## 3. Follow-ups and splits
### work file-followup
Files a bead for work that follows from yours, parented to it.
Use when: You found work you must not do now. A builder child needs a body with a `## Build` section naming the artifact and where.
Arguments:
  "<title>"
  --repo <name>   (required: where the child's work lives)
  --body-file -   (body on stdin)
Checks:
  - the parent is your bead
  - the child never carries your `branch:` label
Refusals:
  - --for is the client's to send (it files as your own persona)
    Fix: drop --for

### work split
Files a bead for a piece of your own bead's scope, parented to it.
Use when: Part of this bead's scope is better landed on its own.
Arguments:
  "<title>"
  --repo <name>   (required)
  --body-file -
Checks:
  - the parent is your bead
  - the child never carries your `branch:` label

## 4. The law
### work statute-check
Checks a plan against the statutes before a governed decision and records the plan and the slugs on the bead.
Use when: Before you add a program, service, daemon or unit; a language or file type; a dependency; or a protocol. A plan that adds none records nothing.
Arguments:
  "<one-paragraph plan>" | -   (plan on stdin)
Checks:
  - the registry and the statute mirror are configured and readable
  - every statute returned has its full text
  - the note is recorded before the text is printed
Refusals:
  - usage: work statute-check "<one-paragraph plan>" | work statute-check - < plan
    Fix: pass exactly one argument: the plan, or `-` with the plan on stdin
  - reading stdin: {err}
    Fix: pass the plan as an argument instead
  - refusing: {err}
    Fix: the statute settings are missing from your environment; report it in a note and exit non-zero
  - refusing: SPIRA_STATUTE_GUARDS and SPIRA_STATUTE_DIR must both be set; an unconfigured check is not a pass
    Fix: do not proceed as if checked; report the missing settings in a note and exit non-zero
  - refusing: cannot read the statute registry {path}: {err}
    Fix: the check cannot run; report it in a note and exit non-zero rather than proceeding unchecked
  - refusing: {err}
    Fix: a returned statute has no text in the mirror; report it in a note, the check is incomplete
  - the note was not recorded on the bead (exit {code}): {err}
    Fix: retry; an unrecorded check does not count

### work law
Prints the statute code from the `law/` tree of your worktree: the table of contents, one part, one numbered section, or one statute by slug.
Use when: Before a choice the statutes govern, to read the rule itself rather than a summary of it.
Arguments:
  [<part> | <part>.<n> | <slug>]   (default: the table of contents)
Checks:
  - it reads the tracked tree of the worktree it runs in; no store, no socket
Refusals:
  - usage: work law [<part> | <part>.<n> | <slug>]
    Fix: pass at most one argument
  - {err}
    Fix: pick a part, section or slug from the table of contents printed with it

## 5. Verifying
Run suites only through testenv; `testenv --help` is its manual.

### work crates
Prints the `-p` words for your diff: touched crates plus their direct dependents.
Use when: Before `cargo test`/`build`/`check`: `cargo test $(work crates)`. Empty output means nothing buildable changed. Never use `--workspace`.
Arguments:
  [<base>]   (default local/main)
Checks:
  - run it inside your worktree, where git and cargo can see the diff
Refusals:
  - git diff {base}...HEAD failed
    Fix: check the base ref exists (`local/main` by default) and that you are in your worktree
  - cargo metadata failed
    Fix: run it from inside the workspace
  - {err}
    Fix: the diff could not be mapped to crates; test the crate you edited by name with `cargo test -p <crate>`

## 6. Review
### work review
Launches one read-only reviewer session over your bead's text and your branch's diff, and prints its findings, one `- file:line — what to change` per line. It changes nothing.
Use when: Before you submit. Answer each finding in your submission.
Arguments:
  [--timeout SECONDS]   (default 600)
  [--base REF]   (default local/main)
Checks:
  - the bead's text is read through `work show`
  - the reviewer runs with your persona's configured model
  - a reviewer that outlives the timeout is stopped; exit 124
Refusals:
  - {err}
    Fix: `work review [--timeout SECONDS] [--base REF]`; the timeout is a whole number of seconds, at least 1
  - work show refused (exit {code}): {err}
    Fix: run it from your bead's own session; `work show` must answer first
  - git diff {base}...HEAD failed
    Fix: check the base ref exists (`local/main` by default) and that you are in your worktree
  - refusing: {err}
    Fix: the reviewer's settings are missing from your environment; report it in a note and submit without a review
  - refusing: SPIRA_AGENT is not set
    Fix: the reviewer cannot be launched; report it in a note and submit without a review
  - timed out after {secs}s; the reviewer was stopped and no findings were returned
    Fix: retry with a longer `--timeout`, or submit and say no review was returned
  - {err}
    Fix: the reviewer session failed; retry once, then submit and say no review was returned

## 7. Submitting
### work submit
Moves your bead to SUBMITTED; the landing pass certifies and lands it.
Use when: The work is committed on your branch, its commit subject names your bead, and you rebased onto local/main. Do not run the full gate first.
Arguments:
  --met "<measured value and where>" | --contradicted "<evidence>" | --short "<value>" with --follow-on <id> or --escalation <id>   (only when the description carries a `Target:` line)
Checks:
  - the tip is read from your branch, never taken from you
  - a bead with a `Target:` line is submitted with a verdict; a submission without one is refused by the broker
  - a --short verdict names a follow-on or escalation you filed
Refusals:
  - no tip could be read from the worktree
    Fix: run it from your worktree after committing; your branch is spira/<bead>
  - {flag} is read from the worktree, never taken from the caller
    Fix: drop {flag}; commit and run `work submit` bare

### work done
Closes a bead whose deliverable is not code on this branch (child beads, a document, mail).
Use when: You produced something other than a commit.
Arguments:
  --delivers "<what you produced, or where it lives>"
  verdict flags as for submit
Checks:
  - the bead is the bound one
  - a bead with a `Target:` line carries a verdict

### work superseded-by
Requests that your bead be retired because another bead already landed its work. Holds it; does not close it.
Use when: You verified the successor's commit is on local/main (`git log local/main | grep <id>`).
Arguments:
  <successor-id>
Checks:
  - the successor is the only other bead you may name

## 8. Other verbs (reach depends on your persona)
The broker refuses a verb your persona's allow row does not name; that refusal is its own text.

### work read
Prints another bead.
Use when: You need a bead your own names (a dependency, a parent).
Arguments:
  <bead-id>
Checks:
  - the broker's allow row for your persona names this verb

### work list
Lists beads matching a filter.
Use when: You need to find work by state or label.
Arguments:
  filter flags as the persona allows
Checks:
  - the broker's allow row for your persona names this verb

### work search
Searches beads by text.
Use when: You are looking for a bead whose id you do not have.
Arguments:
  <text>
Checks:
  - the broker's allow row for your persona names this verb

### work ask
Posts an ask to the operator's mailbox.
Use when: A decision outside your bead needs a human; always give a default and a class basis.
Arguments:
  --subject <q>
  --kind question
  --class <permissions|policy|destructive|design>
  --default <what you would do>
  --body-file -
Checks:
  - the broker's allow row for your persona names this verb

### work note-on
Leaves a note on another bead.
Use when: A lane persona annotates work it does not own.
Arguments:
  <bead-id>
  <text>
Checks:
  - the broker's allow row for your persona names this verb

### work label-add
Adds a label to a bead.
Use when: A lane persona classifies work.
Arguments:
  <bead-id>
  <label>
Checks:
  - the broker's allow row for your persona names this verb

### work label-remove
Removes a label from a bead.
Use when: A lane persona reclassifies work.
Arguments:
  <bead-id>
  <label>
Checks:
  - the broker's allow row for your persona names this verb

### work dep-add
Adds a dependency between beads.
Use when: A lane persona records a real blocking edge.
Arguments:
  <bead-id>
  <depends-on-id>
Checks:
  - the broker's allow row for your persona names this verb

### work relate
Relates two beads without blocking.
Use when: A lane persona links related work.
Arguments:
  <bead-id>
  <other-id>
Checks:
  - the broker's allow row for your persona names this verb

### work reopen
Reopens a bead.
Use when: A lane persona returns work to the queue.
Arguments:
  <bead-id>
Checks:
  - the broker's allow row for your persona names this verb

### work close-other
Closes another bead with a reason.
Use when: A lane persona retires work.
Arguments:
  <bead-id>
  <reason>
Checks:
  - the broker's allow row for your persona names this verb

### work close-ask
Closes an ask in the operator mailbox.
Use when: The decision that settled it is known.
Arguments:
  <ask-id>
  <what decided it>
Checks:
  - the broker's allow row for your persona names this verb

### work file
Files a bead outside any parent.
Use when: A lane persona with a remit to file.
Arguments:
  <title>
  --kind <kind>
  --body-file -
Checks:
  - the broker's allow row for your persona names this verb

### work groom
Runs the groomer.
Use when: Grooming persona only.
Arguments:
  <sub>
Checks:
  - the broker's allow row for your persona names this verb

### work incident
Runs the incident tool.
Use when: Operations persona only.
Arguments:
  <sub>
Checks:
  - the broker's allow row for your persona names this verb

### work sop
Reads or writes standing procedures.
Use when: Operations persona only.
Arguments:
  <sub>
Checks:
  - the broker's allow row for your persona names this verb

### work census
Runs the census.
Use when: Operations persona only.
Checks:
  - the broker's allow row for your persona names this verb

### work queue
Runs queue steps.
Use when: Queue persona only.
Arguments:
  <sub>
Checks:
  - the broker's allow row for your persona names this verb

### work landing-pass
Runs the landing pass.
Use when: Landing persona only.
Arguments:
  <sub>
Checks:
  - the broker's allow row for your persona names this verb

### work strand
Runs the stranded-work tool.
Use when: Operations persona only.
Arguments:
  <sub>
Checks:
  - the broker's allow row for your persona names this verb

### work fence
Runs the fence tool.
Use when: Operations persona only.
Arguments:
  <sub>
Checks:
  - the broker's allow row for your persona names this verb

### work hel
Runs the hel tool.
Use when: Operations persona only.
Arguments:
  <sub>
Checks:
  - the broker's allow row for your persona names this verb

### work mender
Runs the mender tool.
Use when: Mender persona only.
Arguments:
  diagnose|outcome <path> ... (text on stdin)
Checks:
  - the broker's allow row for your persona names this verb

## Rules every verb shares
### work (any verb)
Rules every verb shares.
Use when: Always.
Checks:
  - the verb is one `work` serves
  - no argument is `--actor` or `--stdin`: the client sends those itself
  - a bound verb runs only inside a bead's environment and names no bead but the bound one
  - at most one body (`--body-file`, `--reason-file`, `--why`, or a lone `-`) per request
  - the socket answers; a verb that cannot reach it exits 2 ("cannot tell"), which is safe to retry
Refusals:
  - unknown verb {verb:?} (want {want})
    Fix: use one of the verbs listed; `work prime` shows them all
  - {flag} is the client's to send, never the caller's
    Fix: drop {flag}; the client supplies your persona and the request body itself
  - this environment is not bound to a bead (SPIRA_WORK_BEAD_ID), and {verb} acts on the bound bead
    Fix: run it from a bead's own session, or use a verb that names its target
  - {foreign} is not the bound bead ({bound})
    Fix: name only your own bead; `superseded-by` and the `--follow-on`/`--escalation` values of submit and done are the only places another bead belongs
  - more than one body in one request{detail}
    Fix: put one body on stdin or in one file; fold the rest into it
  - {err}
    Fix: check the path you passed to the body flag, or pipe the text on stdin
  - cannot tell: SPIRA_WORK_BEAD_ID is not set — this environment is not bound to a bead
    Fix: run inside the bead's session; exit 2 means nothing was done and a retry is safe
  - cannot tell: {err}
    Fix: nothing was recorded; retry. If it persists, leave a note with the evidence and exit non-zero
